# CyberGurdian AI — Cursor Rules for V1.7

## Project Identity
This is CyberGurdian AI — an External Trust Intelligence Platform.
NOT a scanner. NOT a vulnerability tool. A TRUST INTELLIGENCE PLATFORM.

- Version: V1.7 (in development)
- Theme: Trust Intelligence + Honest Security + Saudi Compliance Awareness
- Language: Bilingual (English + Arabic) — both first-class
- Source of truth for version: lib/brand.ts

## Sacred Filter (apply to EVERY change)
Before any code change, ask: "Does this increase Trust Clarity?"
- YES → proceed
- MAYBE → don't add
- NO → reject

## Critical Rules

### Rule 1: Server-Only Boundaries
These MUST have `import "server-only"` at top:
- lib/scanner.ts
- lib/claude.ts
- lib/scanToken.ts
- lib/validator.ts
- lib/reputation.ts
- All files in app/api/

NEVER expose these to client bundle.

### Rule 2: Bilingual Data Model
All user-facing strings: `{ en: string, ar: string }`
This includes findings, errors, remediation, UI strings.
NEVER hardcode English-only strings in user-facing components.

### Rule 3: TypeScript Strict
- NEVER use `any` or `as any`
- NEVER use `@ts-ignore` or `@ts-expect-error`
- Strict mode is non-negotiable

### Rule 4: Scoring Engine is SACRED
Files in lib/scoring/ are deterministic and audit-grade.
- DO NOT modify without explicit approval from user
- DO NOT change scoring weights
- DO NOT add new scoring categories

### Rule 5: Observable Coverage Philosophy
"What we cannot see ≠ what is broken."
- Never penalize sites for what's behind CDN
- Always distinguish "full" vs "partial" vs "limited" visibility

### Rule 6: Sprint Discipline
- One sprint at a time
- No drive-by refactors
- Whitelist files per task — don't touch unrelated code
- After every change: tsc --noEmit && npm run lint && npm run build

## Logging — Keep It Simple

When lib/logger.ts is needed, max 20 lines:

const isDev = process.env.NODE_ENV === "development";
export const logger = {
  debug: isDev ? console.log : () => {},
  info: console.log,
  warn: console.warn,
  error: console.error,
};

NO log abstractions. NO transports. NO observability pipelines.

## What NOT to Build in V1.7

FORBIDDEN (belong to V1.8+):
- User authentication
- Database persistence
- Dashboards
- Teams/workspaces
- Billing
- Public shareable report URLs
- Trust badges
- Public API
- Compliance SCORING (only Guidance allowed)
- Port scanning
- Sentry / observability platforms (too early)
- Complex logger abstractions

## Performance Budgets

- Home page First Load JS: < 200 kB after Sprint 1
- Scan completion: < 15 seconds
- PDF generation: < 5 seconds

## Quality Gates (before every commit)

1. npx tsc --noEmit → 0 errors
2. npm run lint → 0 warnings
3. npm run build → succeeds
4. Manual smoke test

## When Unsure, ASK

Before doing ANY of these, ASK the user first:
1. Modifying scoring engine (lib/scoring/)
2. Changing scan token logic (lib/scanToken.ts)
3. Touching SSRF validator (lib/validator.ts)
4. Adding new dependencies (package.json)
5. Modifying middleware
6. Changing API contracts
7. Adding new env variables
8. Refactoring more than 3 files at once

## Commit Format

<type>: <description>

[Optional WHY]

Sprint: V1.7-S1
Task: <number>

Types: feat, fix, refactor, docs, test, chore, perf


